# Claude Code project guide — {ContextName}

This project was bootstrapped by **Genesis v{GenesisVersion}** from bundle `{BundleId}`. The generated domain code marks unfilled business logic with runtime holes (`throw GenesisTODO.notImplemented(...)`) — the project **compiles**, but the test for each unfilled feature is **RED** until you fill it (intentional, TDD-first: write the test, see it red, implement, watch it go green, one feature at a time). `grep GenesisTODO.notImplemented` to find unfilled holes; fill them by saying the domain logic and letting `/domain-fill` write it. Business-logic guidance lives in `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/`.

### Two gates: `swift test` (domain done?) vs `genesis verify` (scaffold honest?)

- **`swift test`** — every generated test starts **RED**. *R-SUT red*: the SUT throws `GenesisTODO` until `/domain-fill` fills the domain. *R-ARRANGE red*: a `/test-gen` JUDGMENT-lane test still has a `genesisTODO("GENESIS-ARRANGE: …")` thunk standing in for its Given — author the Given (fixture call + input value + a positive-arrange `_ = try #require(try await repository.find(byId:))`) in `<UC>Tests.authored.swift`. Needs **localhost KurrentDB** up, or the whole suite is red (DB-down is indistinguishable from unfilled).
- **`genesis verify`** — the honesty gate; **wire it into CI** (fail on exit 2) so unfilled logic cannot ship. It is RED throughout development and goes green only when ALL of: no `GenesisTODO` markers in `Sources/`, every declared invariant has an **enabled live-assertion** test (a `.<code>` token inside a thunk message or comment does NOT count), and no `genesisTODO(` arrange-thunk remains in any enabled `@Test`. If `genesis verify` is green but a `swift test` is still red → the Given is semantically wrong (review it) or localhost KDB is down — not an unfilled hole.

## How to fill in business logic

Use Claude Code skills to materialise stubs:

- `/test-gen <task-spec.md>` — emit REAL (enabled-red) Swift Testing tests for a use case (write the test first, watch it go red→green as you fill). Two-file split: `<UC>Tests.generated.swift` (machine-owned) + `<UC>Tests.authored.swift` (you author JUDGMENT-lane Givens here).
- `/domain-fill [<Aggregate>]` — fill the entity-layer `GenesisTODO.notImplemented` domain holes (command precondition + build-event / non-type-closure `when` reducer / create-init derived fields) by dictating the domain logic; turns R-SUT-red tests green.
- `/usecase <UseCase>` — fill the body of `{UC}Service.execute(input:)` and the matching `{Aggregate}+{UC}.swift` command + `when(event:)` overloads.
- `/readmodel <ReadModel>` — generate read-model projector files (not yet supported by this scaffold; Phase 1.c does not write `projection-model.yaml`).
- `/api` — emit transport adapter from `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/api.md`.

## SSOT discipline

| Master file | Who edits |
|-------------|-----------|
| `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/commands.md` | `/usecase` skill + human |
| `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/invariants.md` | `/usecase` skill + human |
| `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/errors.md` | human |
| `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/api.md` | `/api` skill + human |
| `.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/<Aggregate>/test-scenarios.md` | human (Gherkin master) |

Files in `docs/tasks/` whose frontmatter has `useCase:` are generated by Genesis `/bundle-plan`: don't hand-edit their body; to change one, re-run `/bundle-plan --replan`. Their `status:` field is updated by the workflow (`/bundle-plan`, `/pickup`). Other files in `docs/tasks/` are hand-written specs and can be edited directly. Code files marked `GENESIS-GENERATED` are regenerated wholesale; never hand-edit them.

## Adapter

`./.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/swift-adapter.yaml` controls Genesis behaviour on re-generation (type mapping, custom types, ID strategy, etc.). Schema: see Genesis's `architecture.md §6.2`.

## Manifest

`./.ai/contexts/{ContextName}/.scaffold-manifest.json` records the sha256 of every file Genesis generated. Used by `genesis diff` (Phase 3) to detect human edits and decide which changes are safe to auto-apply.
